\section{Task Formulation}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Problem Definition}
\label{sec:method:problem}

Let $f_\theta$ denote a language model and $\mathcal{D}_0$ a corpus of human-written seed text.
We define a \emph{recursive generation chain} of depth $K$ as the sequence $\mathcal{D}_0, \mathcal{D}_1, \dots, \mathcal{D}_K$, where each $\mathcal{D}_k$ ($k \geq 1$) is produced by finetuning $f_\theta$ on $\mathcal{D}_{k-1}$ and sampling from the resulting model.
Depth $d{=}0$ denotes human-written text; depth $d{=}k$ ($k \geq 1$) denotes text generated by a model trained on depth-$(k{-}1)$ data.

Given a text passage $x$, the task is to estimate its recursive generation depth $d \in \{0, 1, \dots, K\}$.
This is an ordinal classification problem: depths carry a natural ordering, and $d{=}1$ is structurally closer to $d{=}0$ than $d{=}3$ is, since each generation step compounds distributional drift.

\subsection{Multi-Generational Benchmark Construction}
\label{sec:method:benchmark}

We construct a benchmark spanning three axes: model family, decoding strategy, and generation depth.

\paragraph{Model families.}
We select three 1--1.5B-parameter models with diverse pretraining corpora and architectures: Pythia-1.4B (EleutherAI), OLMo-1B (AI2), and GPT-2~XL (OpenAI, 1.5B).
Restricting to a narrow parameter range isolates the effect of architecture and training data from scale.

\paragraph{Decoding strategies.}
Each model is paired with three sampling methods: nucleus sampling ($p{=}0.95$) \cite{holtzman2020_nucleus}, temperature sampling ($\tau{=}0.9$), and top-$k$ sampling ($k{=}50$).
These span the dominant modes of open-ended generation and induce qualitatively different entropy profiles.

\paragraph{Depth and scale.}
The primary benchmark covers depths d0--d3 across all $3 \times 3 = 9$ model--strategy cells, with 5{,}000 samples per depth per cell (180K samples total).
For depth extension experiments (\S\ref{sec:atlas}), we extend one pipeline (Pythia-1.4B, nucleus) to d0--d5.

\paragraph{Generation pipeline.}
Starting from a Wikipedia subset as seed text ($\mathcal{D}_0$), each generation cycle proceeds in three steps: (1)~LoRA finetuning \cite{hu2022lora} with rank $r{=}16$, $\alpha{=}32$, learning rate $2{\times}10^{-4}$, batch size 8, for 3 epochs; (2)~generation of 5{,}000 passages (max 512 tokens) under the specified decoding strategy; (3)~the generated corpus becomes $\mathcal{D}_k$ and serves as training data for the next cycle.
LoRA targets \texttt{q\_proj} and \texttt{v\_proj} for OLMo, and \texttt{c\_attn} and \texttt{c\_proj} for GPT-2-family models.

\paragraph{Reference model.}
All statistical features (\S\ref{sec:method:features}) are computed using a fixed reference model---GPT-2 base (124M)---that is independent of the three generation models.
The reference model computes per-token perplexity for each passage, providing a consistent measurement frame across model families.
In cross-model leave-one-out evaluation (\S\ref{sec:generalization}), the reference model remains fixed even when GPT-2~XL is held out; the partial architectural overlap this introduces is discussed in \S\ref{sec:discussion}.

\subsection{Statistical Feature Design}
\label{sec:method:features}

We extract a 19-dimensional feature vector $\mathbf{f}(x)$ from the reference-model perplexity distribution over each passage $x$.
The features fall into four categories (Table~\ref{tab:features}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{The 19 statistical features comprising $\mathbf{f}(x)$, grouped by category. All perplexity and surprisal features are computed from the reference model (GPT-2 base, 124M).}
\label{tab:features}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llr@{}}
\toprule
Category & Features & $n$ \\
\midrule
Perplexity & mean, var, skewness, kurtosis, & 9 \\
statistics & p10, p25, p50, p75, p90 & \\
\addlinespace
Surprisal & mean, var, entropy & 3 \\
statistics & & \\
\addlinespace
Lexical & type--token ratio, hapax ratio, & 4 \\
diversity & bigram entropy, trigram entropy & \\
\addlinespace
Repetition & repeated 2/3/4-gram fractions & 3 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\emph{Perplexity statistics} (9 features) capture the location, spread, and shape of the per-token perplexity distribution via its mean, variance, skewness, kurtosis, and five percentile quantiles (p10--p90).
The tail quantiles (p75, p90) prove especially informative: recursive finetuning erodes the high-perplexity tail, making these features monotonically decreasing with depth (\S\ref{sec:atlas}).

\emph{Surprisal statistics} (3 features) operate on $-\log p(\text{token})$ and include mean surprisal, surprisal variance, and the entropy of the surprisal distribution.
While correlated with perplexity features, surprisal variance captures second-order structure---the variability of token predictability---that is not redundant with perplexity moments.

\emph{Lexical diversity} (4 features) measures vocabulary usage through type--token ratio, hapax ratio (fraction of tokens appearing exactly once), and bigram/trigram entropy.

\emph{Repetition} (3 features) quantifies the fraction of repeated $n$-grams for $n \in \{2, 3, 4\}$, capturing the increased self-repetition characteristic of recursive generation.

\subsection{Effective Discriminable Depth}
\label{sec:method:edd}

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\subsection{Implications}
\label{sec:discussion:implications}

\paragraph{Depth-graduated data curation.}
Binary synthetic-data filters discard all machine-generated text indiscriminately, yet first-generation outputs may be benign or even beneficial for training \cite{drayson2025_detection_prevents_collapse}.
Our depth estimates offer a finer-grained alternative: predicted depth can serve as a continuous quality proxy for importance weighting in data curation pipelines such as DSIR \cite{dsir2023} and QuRating \cite{qurating2024}.
Rather than filtering at a binary threshold, a curation system could assign importance weights that decay with estimated depth \cite{sugiyama2007_importance_weighting}, retaining shallow-generation text while down-weighting deeply recursive samples.
We note this as a conceptual contribution---empirical validation of depth-weighted curation remains future work.

\paragraph{Feature evolution tracks tail erosion.}
The feature importance analysis in \S\ref{sec:atlas:features} reveals a depth-dependent shift in which features carry discriminative power.
At shallow depths (d0 vs.\ d1), \textsc{var\_surprisal} dominates, reflecting changes in second-order distributional structure---the variability of token surprisals decreases as the model's output distribution narrows.
At deeper recursions (d2 vs.\ d3 and beyond), \textsc{p90\_ppl} and kurtosis assume greater importance, capturing changes in the shape of the distribution's tail.
This progression aligns with the tail erosion theory of \citet{shumailov2024_nature}: early recursive generations preserve bulk distributional properties while eroding variability, and deeper generations progressively collapse the tail itself, consistent with the ``tale of tails'' analysis by \citet{dohmatob2024_tale_of_tails}.
The feature importance shift thus provides an empirical counterpart to these theoretical predictions.

\paragraph{Cross-strategy deployment requires strategy identification.}
The temperature-induced d1/d2 AUC inversion (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossstrategy})---as low as 0.13 for GPT-2~XL and 0.25 for OLMo---has practical consequences.
A depth estimation system deployed without knowledge of the target decoding strategy risks systematic reversal of depth predictions for temperature-sampled text.
In deployment, the strategy type must either be known a priori or inferred as a preprocessing step.
Strategy-conditioned classifiers---where a first stage identifies the decoding strategy and a second stage applies the corresponding depth model---are a natural solution, though the feasibility of reliable strategy identification from text alone is itself an open question.

\subsection{Limitations}
\label{sec:discussion:limitations}

\paragraph{Controlled experimental setting.}
All experiments use a fixed seed corpus (Wikipedia), LoRA finetuning, and three model families at 1--1.5B parameter scale.
This controlled design isolates the effect of recursive depth but leaves open how well the approach transfers to mixed-domain seed text, instruction-tuned models, full finetuning, and larger model scales (7B+).
The gap between our controlled setting and the heterogeneous conditions of real-world recursive contamination remains uncharacterized.

\paragraph{Reference model circularity.}
Our 19 statistical features are computed relative to a GPT-2 base (124M) reference model.
In the cross-model leave-one-out evaluation, the GPT-2~XL holdout shares partial architectural overlap with this reference---both belong to the GPT-2 family.
However, GPT-2~XL holdout accuracy (67.6\%) does not exceed that of the architecturally unrelated OLMo holdout (69.2\%), suggesting that architectural overlap is not the primary driver of transfer performance.
Nonetheless, the choice of reference model introduces a potential confound that warrants investigation with non-GPT-2 reference models.

\paragraph{Feature fragility under domain shift.}
\citet{pudasaini2026_detection_fails} demonstrate that detection features degrade under domain shift, with classifiers trained on one text domain failing to generalize to others.
Our ordinal depth features---derived from the same class of perplexity and entropy statistics---are likely susceptible to analogous fragility.
A depth estimator trained on Wikipedia-seeded recursive text may not transfer to recursive generation seeded from news, code, or dialogue without domain-specific recalibration.

\paragraph{Non-monotonic early-depth dynamics.}
Our EDD framework assumes that text quality degrades monotonically with recursive depth.
\citet{bakshi2026_collapse_improvement} present evidence that early recursive iterations can temporarily \emph{improve} model quality before eventual collapse, implying that the depth--degradation relationship may be non-monotonic in the initial generations.
If feature trajectories reflect this non-monotonicity, the ordinal classification assumption---that depth classes are naturally ordered---may break down for shallow depths under certain conditions.

\paragraph{Curated synthetic pipelines.}
\citet{falahati2026_curated} show that carefully curated synthetic data does not necessarily trigger model collapse, challenging the assumption that recursive depth invariably produces distributional degradation.
In such curated pipelines, the statistical fingerprints we exploit may be absent or attenuated, rendering depth estimation infeasible.
The boundary conditions under which curation neutralizes depth signal remain unknown.
